Now let's understand the flow

There are two separate flows.

FLOW 1 — Registration

You send:

POST /api/auth/register

Body:

{
    "email": "krishna@gmail.com",
    "password": "admin24"
}
Starting point

The flow starts here:

@PostMapping("/register")
public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<String>> register(...)

inside:

AuthController

Then:

AuthController
      ↓
authService.register(request)

Then:

AuthServiceImpl
      ↓
passwordEncoder.encode("admin24")

Suppose BCrypt produces:

$2a$10$abc123........

Then:

UserSecurity
email    = krishna@gmail.com
password = $2a$10$abc123........
role     = USER

Then:

userSecurityRepository.save(user);

Finally:

PostgreSQL

Database:

id | email              | password               | role
---+--------------------+------------------------+------
1  | krishna@gmail.com  | $2a$10$abc123......... | USER

The plain password admin24 is never stored.

FLOW 2 — Login

Now you send:

POST /api/auth/login

Body:

{
    "email": "krishna@gmail.com",
    "password": "admin24"
}
Starting point

Again, the first point in your application code is:

@PostMapping("/login")

Then:

AuthController
       ↓
AuthServiceImpl

Your service executes:

authenticationManager.authenticate(
    new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
        request.getEmail(),
        request.getPassword()
    )
);

This is where you hand authentication over to Spring Security.

From here Spring Security takes over
AuthServiceImpl
      │
      ▼
AuthenticationManager
      │
      ▼
ProviderManager
      │
      ▼
DaoAuthenticationProvider

Your SecurityConfig created:

DaoAuthenticationProvider provider =
        new DaoAuthenticationProvider(userDetailsService);

So the provider knows:

"When I need user information, call UserSecurityService."

Therefore:

DaoAuthenticationProvider
        ↓
UserSecurityService

It calls:

loadUserByUsername("krishna@gmail.com")
Then your database is accessed

Your code:

return userSecurityRepository
        .findByEmail(email)

runs.

Conceptually:

SELECT *
FROM user_security
WHERE email = 'krishna@gmail.com';

Database returns:

email = krishna@gmail.com
password = $2a$10$abc123........
role = USER

Because your entity implements:

UserDetails

Spring Security can directly use this object.

Then password verification happens

Spring now has two things:

User entered
admin24
Database
$2a$10$abc123........

Your:

PasswordEncoder

is:

new BCryptPasswordEncoder()

Spring performs the equivalent of:

passwordEncoder.matches(
    "admin24",
    "$2a$10$abc123........"
);

If correct:

true

Authentication succeeds.

If wrong:

false

Authentication fails.

Complete flow

Remember this diagram:

                REGISTER
                   │
                   ▼
            AuthController
                   │
                   ▼
            AuthServiceImpl
                   │
                   ▼
        BCryptPasswordEncoder
                   │
                   ▼
             UserSecurity
                   │
                   ▼
           UserSecurityRepository
                   │
                   ▼
               DATABASE

Then login:

                  LOGIN
                    │
                    ▼
             AuthController
                    │
                    ▼
             AuthServiceImpl
                    │
                    ▼
          AuthenticationManager
                    │
                    ▼
             ProviderManager
                    │
                    ▼
        DaoAuthenticationProvider
                    │
                    ▼
          UserSecurityService
                    │
                    ▼
        UserSecurityRepository
                    │
                    ▼
                DATABASE
                    │
                    ▼
             UserSecurity
                    │
                    ▼
        BCryptPasswordEncoder
                    │
              matches()
               /      \
             TRUE     FALSE
              │         │
              ▼         ▼
          SUCCESS      ERROR
The most important distinction

Registration:

passwordEncoder.encode()

Login:

authenticationManager.authenticate()

You should not do this during login:

passwordEncoder.encode(request.getPassword())

and you should not do:

user.getPassword().equals(request.getPassword())

Spring Security's DaoAuthenticationProvider handles the BCrypt comparison for you.